latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Check for uniformity:\n   - Iterate through all cells in the input grid.\n   - If all cells have the same color, return the input grid unchanged.\n\n2. Analyze grid complexity:\n   - Initialize a set to store unique color pairs.\n   - Iterate through the grid, checking each cell with its right and bottom neighbors.\n   - Add each unique color pair (including same-color pairs) to the set.\n\n3. Determine expansion factor:\n   - If the number of unique color pairs is 2 or 3, set expansion factor to 2.\n   - If the number of unique color pairs is 4 or 5, set expansion factor to 3.\n   - If the number of unique color pairs is 6 or more, set expansion factor to 4.\n\n4. Create expanded grid:\n   - Calculate new dimensions by multiplying original dimensions by the expansion factor.\n   - Initialize a new ColoredGrid with the calculated dimensions.\n\n5. Fill expanded grid:\n   - Iterate through each cell in the original grid.\n   - For each cell:\n     a. Get the color of the current cell.\n     b. Calculate the top-left corner of the expanded region for this cell.\n     c. Fill a square region in the new grid with the cell's color.\n        The square size should be equal to the expansion factor.\n\n6. Return the expanded grid.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of each step:\n\n1. Checking for uniformity:\n   ```python\n   def is_uniform(grid):\n       first_color = grid.get_cell(0, 0)\n       rows, cols = grid.get_dimensions()\n       return all(grid.get_cell(r, c) == first_color for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols))\n   ```\n\n2. Analyzing grid complexity:\n   ```python\n   def analyze_complexity(grid):\n       unique_pairs = set()\n       rows, cols = grid.get_dimensions()\n       for r in range(rows):\n           for c in range(cols):\n               color = grid.get_cell(r, c)\n               if c < cols - 1:\n                   right_color = grid.get_cell(r, c + 1)\n                   unique_pairs.add((color, right_color))\n               if r < rows - 1:\n                   bottom_color = grid.get_cell(r + 1, c)\n                   unique_pairs.add((color, bottom_color))\n       return len(unique_pairs)\n   ```\n\n3. Determining expansion factor:\n   ```python\n   def get_expansion_factor(complexity):\n       if complexity <= 3:\n           return 2\n       elif complexity <= 5:\n           return 3\n       else:\n           return 4\n   ```\n\n4. Creating expanded grid:\n   ```python\n   def create_expanded_grid(original_grid, expansion_factor):\n       rows, cols = original_grid.get_dimensions()\n       new_rows, new_cols = rows * expansion_factor, cols * expansion_factor\n       return ColoredGrid(values=[[0 for _ in range(new_cols)] for _ in range(new_rows)])\n   ```\n\n5. Filling expanded grid:\n   ```python\n   def fill_expanded_grid(original_grid, expanded_grid, expansion_factor):\n       rows, cols = original_grid.get_dimensions()\n       for r in range(rows):\n           for c in range(cols):\n               color = original_grid.get_cell(r, c)\n               for dr in range(expansion_factor):\n                   for dc in range(expansion_factor):\n                       expanded_grid.set_cell(r * expansion_factor + dr, \n                                              c * expansion_factor + dc, \n                                              color)\n   ```\n\n6. Main function:\n   ```python\n   def solve_d4b1c2b1(input_grid):\n       if is_uniform(input_grid):\n           return input_grid\n       \n       complexity = analyze_complexity(input_grid)\n       expansion_factor = get_expansion_factor(complexity)\n       expanded_grid = create_expanded_grid(input_grid, expansion_factor)\n       fill_expanded_grid(input_grid, expanded_grid, expansion_factor)\n       \n       return expanded_grid\n   ```\n\nThis plan should correctly handle all the given examples and test cases, adapting the expansion factor based on the complexity of the input grid."
